              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**AltaVista (altavista.com)** was one of the most popular and pioneering early search engines of the World Wide Web. \n\nHere is a quick look at its history, rise, and eventual fall:\n\n### 1. The Rise (1995\u20131997)\n* **Origins:** Launched on December 15, 1995, AltaVista was created by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC). It was initially designed as a showcase to demonstrate the raw power of DEC's Alpha 64-bit database servers. \n* **Innovations:** It was the first \"full-text\" searchable index of the web. Before AltaVista, search directories (like the original Yahoo!) were curated manually. AltaVista used an incredibly fast web crawler named \"Scooter\" that could index millions of pages far quicker than anything else at the time.\n* **Instant Success:** On its first day, the site received roughly 300,000 hits; within a couple of years, it was processing over 80 million queries a day, making it the premier search engine of the late 1990s. It is also credited with pioneering early translation services (Babel Fish) and implementing the first-ever CAPTCHA to stop spam bots.\n\n### 2. The Decline (1998\u20132000s)\n* **Corporate Distraction:** In 1998, Compaq bought DEC, and the management of AltaVista became messy. In an effort to copy Yahoo!'s massive success at the time, Compaq decided to turn the clean, fast AltaVista search engine into a bloated \"web portal\" complete with shopping, email, and heavy banner advertisements. \n* **The Rise of Google:** While AltaVista was busy cluttering its homepage, Google launched with a stripped-back, lightning-fast design and a vastly superior algorithm (PageRank). Users quickly migrated to Google for its cleaner interface and higher-quality search results. \n\n### 3. Acquisition and Shut Down (2003\u20132013)\n* **Yahoo Purchase:** After bouncing through several owners (including CMGI), AltaVista was acquired by Overture Services, which was subsequently bought by **Yahoo!** in 2003. \n* **The End of an Era:** Yahoo kept the brand alive for a decade, though it ran entirely on Yahoo's backend technology. On **July 8, 2013**, Yahoo officially shut AltaVista down forever. \n\n### Current Status\nIf you type **altavista.com** into your browser today, you will automatically be redirected to **Yahoo! Search**.",
